{"id":"hebron","name":"Hebron","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","easton"],"topics":["nave:hebron"],"types":[{"type":"place","from":"places atlas"},{"type":"person","from":"UBS: Names of People"}],"standing":"single-index","indexes":["nave"],"thin":false,"groups":["2. A city of the tribe of Judah, south of Jerusalem"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Hebron","text":"A community; alliance. (1.) A city in the south end of the valley of Eshcol, about midway between Jerusalem and Beersheba, from which it is distant about 20 miles in a straight line. It was built “seven years before Zoan in Egypt” (Gen. 13:18; Num. 13:22). It still exists under the same name, and is one of the most ancient cities in the world. Its earlier name was Kirjath-arba (Gen. 23:2; Josh. 14:15; 15:3). But “Hebron would appear to have been the original name of the city, and it was not till after Abraham’s stay there that it received the name Kirjath-arba, who [i.e., Arba] was not the founder but the conqueror of the city, having led thither the tribe of the Anakim, to which he belonged. It retained this name till it came into the possession of Caleb, when the Israelites restored the original name Hebron” (Keil, Com.). The name of this city does not occur in any of the prophets or …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H2275","lemma":"חֶבְרוֹן","xlit":"Chebrôwn","gloss":"Chebron, a place in Palestine, also the name of two Israelites","kjv":"Hebron","count":35,"share":0.4},{"strong":"H5221","lemma":"נָכָה","xlit":"nâkâh","gloss":"to strike","kjv":"beat, cast forth, clap, give","count":12,"share":0.14},{"strong":"H6310","lemma":"פֶּה","xlit":"peh","gloss":"the mouth , whether literal or figurative","kjv":"accord, after, appointment, assent","count":10,"share":0.11},{"strong":"H4428","lemma":"מֶלֶךְ","xlit":"melek","gloss":"a king","kjv":"king, royal","count":20,"share":0.23},{"strong":"H5927","lemma":"עָלָה","xlit":"ʻâlâh","gloss":"to ascend, intransitively or actively","kjv":"arise, ascend up, at once, break",